This repository is signed. provenance/manifest.json lists a SHA-256 hash
for every generated artifact (model bundles, cleaned datasets, report
figures, dashboard) and carries an Ed25519 signature over that list, made
with a private key kept outside this repository; the matching public key
is provenance/pubkey.pem. Running nightingale.provenance.verify() against
a checkout recomputes each hash from the files actually on disk and checks
the signature, so a downloaded or altered copy can be told apart from the
author's own.

provenance/pubkey.pem ships inside this repository, so it is only trustworthy
if you got it from somewhere you already trust — a checkout re-signed by
someone else, with their own key committed in its place, will verify
against itself just fine. To check that the key actually in front of you is
the author's, compare it out-of-band (e.g. against the GitHub repository
page, or this NOTICE as published by the author) against its fingerprint,
the SHA-256 of the raw 32-byte Ed25519 public key:

  49e4e52d6015eb7398cc04b0fd8fcb8fc5a82cd3a501836af55bb15518bd17a8

Every models/<slug>/model.json also carries a canaries block: deterministic
inputs and the probability this project's own code gives them. Given a lone
model.json found elsewhere, nightingale.provenance.fingerprint() identifies
which of the six published conditions it is a copy of — even renamed, with
its provenance metadata removed — by running each condition's own reference
canary inputs through both the reference's trees and the candidate's trees
and comparing the pre-calibration outputs, rather than trusting anything
the candidate file supplies about itself.
